WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
This dual panel flow chart shows two very different app failures.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
On the left, an app loads successfully, though it's missing a minor UI component.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.000
On the right, the entire load process halts immediately at a fatal crash screen before anything renders.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The mechanism responsible for whether your app fails gracefully or crashes entirely is the turbo-module registry.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
It access the critical bridge, pulling native code into your JavaScript environment.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Let's say you are building an app that relies on a mandatory native storage module.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
When you go to import it, the registry offers you two distinct loading paths, get and get enforcing.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Making the correct API choice here dictates how your application behaves under stress.

9
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
It determines whether your app will survive an undetected native dependency or fail fatally the moment a user launches it.

10
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Using turbo-module registry.get enforcing, a missing module throws an invariant violation error.

11
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
This evaluation runs at module load time, the exact moment the file imports.

12
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
This stops the app dead before rendering.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
This early timing causes confusing stack traces pointing to a top level import instead of the UI function.

14
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
By failing immediately and loudly, get enforcing serves as an aggressive diagnostic tool.

15
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:27.000
It classifies a missing native module as a severe programming error that must be fixed before shipping, rather than a runtime hiccup.

16
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Alternatively, the get method provides a safe path, outputting null when a module is undetected.

17
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Use this for optional modules, like analytics or web environments.

18
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
However, you must manually write a conditional safety check before execution to prevent a runtime null reference error.

19
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Utilizing the get method shifts the burden of handling missing dependencies away from the react native framework and directly into your own application logic.

20
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Consider a platform specific edge case.

21
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
An active health kit module runs on iOS while Android has an empty slot.

22
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Applying get enforcing universally crashes Android on startup.

23
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
But relying entirely on get, sacrifices strict crash protection guarantees on iOS.

24
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.000
How do you strictly enforce the module on iOS while simultaneously ignoring it safely on Android?

25
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
This code block reveals the solution.

26
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:32.000
By wrapping the call in platform.select, you conditionally apply the strict get enforcing method specifically for the iOS key and map a safe null fallback to the Android key.

27
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Platform aware conditional loading allows you to enforce strict dependencies exactly where they belong without introducing cross-platform startup crashes.

28
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:50.000
If you encounter an invariant violation on a module you know is mandatory, the registry is telling you the binary doesn't match your JavaScript expectations.

29
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.000
You need to start troubleshooting.

30
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
This diagnostics low chart outlines the exact physical checklist required to fix the error.

31
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
First, check that the string name in your JS spec matches the native implementation.

32
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Second, verify the package is registered in your native application code.

33
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
And third, rebuild the native binary entirely.

34
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
This decision matrix table consolidates the entire API selection process.

35
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
It maps your three main scenarios, mandatory, optional, and platform specific directly to their corresponding solutions.

36
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Get enforcing, get, and the combined platform select approach.

37
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Mastering this load time API decision eliminates the risk of silent failures, and ensures robust stability across the entire JavaScript-to-native bridge.

